---
import danHeadshot from "../images/headshots/dan-avatar.png";
import marinaHeadshot from "../images/headshots/Marina-Levy-Headshot.png";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { proof } from "../data/services";
---

<Layout
  title="About | 17th Street Labs"
  description="17th Street Labs is an applied AI engineering and research studio in Denver, New York City, and Boston. Meet the people who build, break, measure, and rebuild intelligent systems."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="chip">About</p>
      <h1 class="statement">We work in the gap between “holy shit, this is possible” and “yes, we can actually ship this.”</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        17th Street Labs is an applied AI engineering and research studio.
        AI is easy to demo. Making it reliable, measurable, useful, secure,
        efficient, and trustworthy enough to do real work is harder. That is
        where we live.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <dl class="proof">
        {proof.map(({ value, label }) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{value}</dt>
            <dd>{label}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="chip">Team</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Senior people, one studio.</h2>
        <p>The people you meet are the people who do the work. Not a committee approving every sentence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="founders">
        <article class="card founder">
          <span class="avatar avatar-portrait avatar-marina">
            <img src={marinaHeadshot.src} alt="Marina Levy" width={800} height={800} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3>Marina Levy</h3>
            <p class="role">Co-founder, chief executive officer</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Marina sets the operating direction for 17th Street Labs. She
              shapes ambitious technical work into focused engagements with a
              clear scope, a steady cadence, and a measurable outcome we can be
              held to. If an engagement is going well, that is usually her
              doing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card founder">
          <span class="avatar avatar-portrait">
            <img src={danHeadshot.src} alt="Dan" width={danHeadshot.width} height={danHeadshot.height} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3>Dan</h3>
            <p class="role">Founding Engineer</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Builds it, breaks it, measures it, builds it again. Dan asks the
              questions that decide whether a system is real: What actually
              happens if we test it? Can we prove this improved? What is the
              cheapest model that can do the job? What happens if we parallelize
              it? Are we evaluating outputs or the whole trajectory? What
              changes when attackers have the same leverage?
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card founder">
          <span class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">KC</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Kyle Coberly</h3>
            <p class="role">Head of Research</p>
            <p class="bio">
              Software, product, and systems engineer with a parallel career as
              an educator. At Urban Sky he designed and shipped the flight
              simulation and training engine, stood up the company-wide testing
              stack, and built the tooling that lets AI agents work in parallel
              across every company app. At Sunrun he led a six-engineer squad
              through a rescue rewrite of the sales-pipeline tool. Adjunct
              professor at the University of Denver and a seven-time Pluralsight
              Elite Instructor.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band-head">
        <p class="chip">Principles</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Visionary and rigorous, at the same time.</h2>
        <p>
          AI should be a first-class citizen in the product, not a chatbot
          bolted onto yesterday’s software. And none of that matters if the
          thing does not work. So we hold both.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principles">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="Gauge" /></span>
          <h3>Stop guessing. Start measuring.</h3>
          <p>
            “Seems better” is not an engineering methodology. Eval-driven
            development turns AI development from vibes into engineering:
            measure what changed, find the failure, fix it, prove the fix
            worked.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="ShieldCheck" /></span>
          <h3>The system is the product.</h3>
          <p>
            The model is a component. Architecture matters. Harnesses matter.
            Context matters. Tools matter. Bigger is not automatically smarter,
            and the smartest architecture may use a smaller model.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="card-icon"><Icon name="Zap" /></span>
          <h3>We experiment on ourselves first.</h3>
          <p>
            We spend our own money discovering what does not work and finding
            surprisingly cheap things that do. What survives becomes
            engineering we use for clients, and your team leaves able to run
            it without us.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="closing">
        <h2 class="statement">Bring us the thing nobody trusts.</h2>
        <p>Send the problem, not a pitch. We will tell you if we can make it work.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-solid" href="/contact/">
            Talk to 17th Street Labs
            <Icon name="ArrowRight" size={16} />
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
